# /etc/pam.d/{{.PamService}}: how sudo authenticates {{.ExecUser}}, and only
# {{.ExecUser}}.
#
# Written by `faramir init --allow-sudo` and REWRITTEN BY EVERY RUN. Re-running
# init without --allow-sudo removes it, along with the sudoers entry that names it.
#
# A service of faramir's own rather than the stack every account's sudo reads,
# selected by {{.SudoersFile}}'s `pam_service` and `pam_login_service`. Everyone
# else's sudo reads the stock stack and is untouched by anything here: a mistake
# below reaches {{.ExecUser}} and nobody else, so it can neither lock this host's
# operators out of sudo nor hand any other account a free one.
#
# ONLY ON A HOST WHOSE SUDO IS THE ORIGINAL. sudo-rs has no pam_service, reaches
# no service a caller may name, and so has no way to be sent here; there the same
# four lines are written into {{.SudoPamFile}} behind a branch on the account,
# and this file is not written at all. See etc/pam.d-sudo.tmpl.
#
# There is no password in this. The authentication step is a helper that asks
# the broker whether the brokered command making this call was approved by a
# human, so nothing is minted, stored or handed out, and an escalation cannot be
# carried to a later command.
#
# THE TWO WORDS THAT MATTER:
#
#   requisite:  not `sufficient`. With `sufficient`, a helper that REFUSES is
#                 not fatal, the stack falls through to pam_permit below, and
#                 every escalation is granted without asking anybody.
#   seteuid:    pam_exec otherwise runs the helper with the REAL uid, which
#                 under setuid sudo is {{.ExecUser}}'s own. The broker answers
#                 the escalate op to root and to nobody else, so without this the
#                 helper is refused and EVERY escalation fails: the feature is
#                 off rather than loose, but it is off. It also keeps the
#                 process doing the deciding out of the reach of the uid being
#                 decided about, instead of resting on the kernel marking a
#                 setuid child non-dumpable, which it does today and which is
#                 not a thing to build a boundary on.
#
# Fail-closed by construction: a missing module, an unparseable line or a
# non-zero helper all end in a refusal, because nothing here authenticates
# except by reaching pam_permit past a requisite that succeeded.
auth     requisite  pam_exec.so quiet seteuid {{.PamHelper}}
#
# pam_env is where {{.SudoEnvFile}} is read, on every host: what PAM puts in the
# environment it hands back is what a brokered command keeps across its sudo.
# sudoers has an env_file that does the same job, but sudo-rs has no such
# setting, and one mechanism that works on both beats two that each work on one.
# `optional`, because a variable that did not arrive is not a reason to grant or
# refuse root -- the decision is the line above. sudo sets PATH, HOME and SUDO_*
# over whatever this names, which is the precedence env_file had.
auth     optional   pam_env.so envfile={{.SudoEnvFile}} readenv=1
auth     required   pam_permit.so
account  required   pam_permit.so
session  required   pam_permit.so
